{
  "id": "ag-quadratics",
  "chapterNumber": "2",
  "title": {
    "en": "Quadratic Functions",
    "ar": null
  },
  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Quadratics introduce curvature. Their U-shaped graphs — parabolas — appear in projectile motion, optimization, and a large share of every standardized test.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Definition 2.1 — Quadratic function",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>A quadratic function has the form \\( f(x)=ax^2+bx+c \\) with \\( a\\neq 0 \\). Its graph is a parabola opening upward when \\( a&gt;0 \\) and downward when \\( a&lt;0 \\), with axis of symmetry \\( x=-\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Theorem 2.2 — The quadratic formula",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>The solutions of \\( ax^2+bx+c=0 \\) are</p>\\[ x=\\frac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}. \\]<p style=\"margin-bottom:0\">The <strong>discriminant</strong> \\( \\Delta=b^2-4ac \\) reveals the roots: \\( \\Delta&gt;0 \\) gives two real roots, \\( \\Delta=0 \\) one repeated root, and \\( \\Delta&lt;0 \\) no real roots.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "In the explorer, \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) reshape the parabola live. The amber vertex sits on the dashed axis of symmetry, and when the discriminant is non-negative the amber roots appear exactly where the curve meets the \\( x \\)-axis.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "canvasId": "quadCanvas",
        "title": {
